From: Banana <bananalinux@proton.me>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 13:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] audio/common/alsa: fall back to the configured latency, not to a crash

A core may ask for more latency than the device's frontend can hold at all: the
PlayStation cores request 192 ms unconditionally through
RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_SET_MINIMUM_AUDIO_LATENCY, and the audioreach frontend on the
Retroid Pocket Classic refuses it. RetroArch treated that as fatal and carried on
with a handle it had never finished configuring, so audio_common's
snd_pcm_avail() asserted a few frames later -- SIGABRT, and a game that never
appears ("PlayStation does not work", for what is an audio negotiation failure).

Falling back to the device's *largest* buffer is worse than it sounds: measured on
the handheld it left the stream at a 30 ms buffer of three periods, and the speaker
amplifiers answered every start attempt with "start failure (-1)" -- the game ran,
the DSP consumed the data, and nothing was heard. The size every working stream
here has always used is the one the user configured (64 ms), so ask for that, and
if even that is refused, ask for nothing and let snd_pcm_hw_params() refine to what
the device supports.

The failure message also stops reporting "Success" where it meant an error: it
printed a stale errnum instead of the call's own result.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/audio/common/alsa.c
+++ b/audio/common/alsa.c
@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@
 
 #include "../audio_driver.h"
 #include "../../verbosity.h"
+#include "../../configuration.h"
 
 int alsa_init_pcm(snd_pcm_t **pcm,
    const char* device,
@@ -141,16 +142,43 @@
    if (new_rate)
       *new_rate = rate;
 
-   if ((snd_pcm_hw_params_set_buffer_time_near(*pcm, params, &latency_usec, NULL)) < 0)
-   {
-      RARCH_ERR("[ALSA] Failed to request a buffer time near %uus for %s device \"%s\": %s.\n",
-            latency_usec,
-            snd_pcm_stream_name(stream),
-            snd_pcm_name(*pcm),
-            snd_strerror(errnum));
-
-      goto error;
-
+   /*
+    * A core can ask for more latency than the device's frontend can hold at all:
+    * the PlayStation cores ask for 192 ms unconditionally (see
+    * RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_SET_MINIMUM_AUDIO_LATENCY), and an audioreach frontend on
+    * a Qualcomm SoC refuses it. Failing here is not graceful: the caller carries on
+    * with a handle it never finished configuring and the driver asserts on
+    * snd_pcm_avail(NULL) a few frames later -- a crash, and a game that never
+    * appears. Falling back to the device's *largest* buffer is worse than it
+    * sounds: measured on the Retroid Pocket Classic it left the stream at a
+    * 30 ms buffer of 3 periods, and the speaker amplifiers then answer every
+    * start attempt with "start failure (-1)" -- the game runs in silence. So ask
+    * for what the user themselves configured (64 ms, the size every working
+    * stream here has been using all along) and, if that is refused too, ask for
+    * nothing and let the device refine to its own default.
+    */
+   if ((errnum = snd_pcm_hw_params_set_buffer_time_near(*pcm, params, &latency_usec, NULL)) < 0)
+   {
+      settings_t *settings     = config_get_ptr();
+      unsigned user_latency_us = settings ? settings->uints.audio_latency * 1000u : 0;
+
+      RARCH_WARN("[ALSA] %s device \"%s\" will not take a %us buffer (%s).\n",
+            snd_pcm_stream_name(stream), snd_pcm_name(*pcm), latency_usec / 1000u,
+            snd_strerror(errnum));
+
+      if (user_latency_us > 0 && user_latency_us / 1000u != latency_usec / 1000u)
+      {
+         latency_usec = user_latency_us;
+         errnum       = snd_pcm_hw_params_set_buffer_time_near(*pcm, params, &latency_usec, NULL);
+         if (errnum == 0)
+            RARCH_WARN("[ALSA] Using the configured %ums buffer instead.\n", latency_usec / 1000u);
+      }
+
+      if (errnum < 0)
+      {
+         RARCH_WARN("[ALSA] Leaving the buffer size to the device.\n");
+         errnum = 0;	/* no buffer asked for at all: snd_pcm_hw_params() picks what it supports */
+      }
    }
 
    if ((errnum = snd_pcm_hw_params_set_periods_near(*pcm, params, &periods, NULL)) < 0)
